"criteria":["G6"],"decision":"block","drop_reason":null,"evidence":["binding_store.py:648-653 — `set_last_get` writes `entry[\"last_get_pass\"] = pass_id`; pass_id advances every pass, so up to 20 inline entries change per pass","Plan Context: 'last_get_pass currently changes up to 20 binding entries per pass even when no real binding changes'","Plan Approach: 'keep writing the legacy inline field in this story so old binaries remain compatible'","AC1: 'An idempotent real-repo pass ... leaves the committed bridge state byte-identical and creates no commit'"],"finding":"The plan keeps writing the inline `last_get_pass` field every pass (via set_last_get, which stamps the advancing pass_id onto up to 20 GET'd entries) while AC1 requires the committed bindings.json to be byte-identical across an idempotent pass. Continued inline writes reproduce the exact churn the story targets, so AC1 cannot hold as specified — the plan never explains how the sidecar absorbs the per-pass rotation churn while inline writes continue.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"fa90d8f269a0f5a43","impact":1.0,"is_primary":null,"location":"Approach section ('keep writing the legacy inline field in this story') vs AC1 ('leaves the committed bridge state byte-identical and creates no commit')","norm_id":"n6711bd5490b3026b","priority":0.8,"reason":"high-priority+criterion-opted-in","scenarios":["Two back-to-back idempotent passes each re-GET the same rotation-selected absent keys; each stamps a new pass_id inline, so bindings.json diffs and AC1's byte-identical/no-commit check fails"],"severity":"critical","suggested_fix":"","tier":"LLM","validity":0.8,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"na","asserted_capability_confirmed":"yes","cited_reference_accurate":"yes","claims_absence":"no","committed_work_relies_on_unbacked_claim":"yes","evidence_entails_finding":"yes","impact_follows_necessarily":"yes","is_verifiable":"yes","no_existing_mitigation":"no","no_viable_alternati